{
  "fromId": "k",
  "toId": "x",
  "preamble": "The lenition or spirantization of a voiceless velar stop /k/ into a velar fricative /x/, a classic example of weakening in the consonant system. Cuzco Quechua spirantizes coda *k to [x] (*suk > hux 'one'). Toda spirantized non-initial Proto-Dravidian *k to x (*pakal 'daylight' > poxol, *pokay 'smoke' > pax).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Spirantization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Iranian",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *krátuš 'power, intellect'",
          "to": "Avestan 𐬑𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬎 xratu"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *kšáp- 'night'",
          "to": "Avestan xšap-",
          "note": "Before a consonant the voiceless velar stop became the fricative x in Iranian."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Khanty to Northern Khanty (Kazym)",
      "languageFamily": "Ob-Ugric",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*kotǝl 'sun'",
          "to": "хăтǝԓ xătəɬ",
          "note": "Northern Khanty *k > x before back vowels; Surgut has ӄ"
        },
        {
          "from": "*kut 'six'",
          "to": "хөт",
          "note": "cf. Surgut ӄут"
        },
        {
          "from": "*kump 'wave'",
          "to": "хумп",
          "note": "cf. Surgut ӄумп"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Mansi to Northern Mansi (Sosva)",
      "languageFamily": "Ob-Ugric",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*kātǝl 'day'",
          "to": "хо̄тал",
          "note": "Northern Mansi *k > x before back vowels; Pelym and Tavda keep k (Tavda котылъ)"
        },
        {
          "from": "*kūl 'fish'",
          "to": "хӯл",
          "note": "cf. Pelym куль"
        },
        {
          "from": "*kūrǝm 'three'",
          "to": "хӯрум",
          "note": "cf. Western Mansi курумъ"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Cuzco Quechua",
      "languageFamily": "Quechua II-C",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/ˈpaʧak/ 'hundred'",
          "to": "[ˈpaʧax]",
          "note": "Syllable-coda allophone; onset /k/ stays a stop (ˈkaʧi 'salt')."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Quechuan *suk 'one'",
          "to": "hux",
          "note": "Ayacucho, which lacks coda spirantization, has huk."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Sakhalin Ainu",
      "languageFamily": "Ainu",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Hokkaido Ainu itak 'language, word' (retains the stop)",
          "to": "itah 'words'",
          "note": "Sakhalin Ainu merged syllable-final /p/, /t/, /k/ and /r/ into /x/, written h; compare kabahuto aynu itah 'Sakhalin Ainu language'."
        },
        {
          "from": "Hokkaido Ainu yuk [júk̚] 'deer'",
          "to": "yuh 'deer'",
          "note": "Hattori's survey gives yúk across Hokkaido and yuh for Karafuto (Sakhalin)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Hokkaido Ainu sak 'summer'",
          "to": "sah 'summer'; sahnoski 'midsummer', sahkes 'late summer'",
          "note": "The Karafuto row also has sakiita 'in summer', where k stays before a vowel, so only the coda is affected."
        },
        {
          "from": "Hokkaido Ainu wakka 'water'",
          "to": "wahka 'water'",
          "note": "The first, syllable-final half of the geminate becomes the fricative."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Tungusic to Manchu",
      "languageFamily": "Jurchenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*akun 'elder brother'",
          "to": "Manchu ahūn",
          "note": "Manchu ⟨h⟩ is /x/, realized [χ] before a, o, ū; Jurchen 阿洪."
